WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.770
- First of all, who is that in your arms?

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.168
- This is Henry. Henry, you gotta say hi.

00:00:06.168 --> 00:00:07.250
Let's say hi to Drew.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.480
- [Drew] And how old is Henry?

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.420
- Henry is almost 12 years old.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.410
- Henry is so cute.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.051
So my dog Douglas here, he's almost 12.

00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:18.350
- Okay.
- And then my dog Lucy, here,

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.120
is gonna be seven.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.000
And so when Douglas had
Lucy walk into his life,

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.484
it was like Claudia Schiffer
walking into his life.

00:00:26.484 --> 00:00:28.260
- All right, yeah.
- So he's a happy guy.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.600
And this is Jeremy, who is a girl.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.310
But she's a bearded dragon. Anyway.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:37.030
- It's not always easy
to tell with those guys.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:38.640
But I love that you're a reptile fan.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.310
The reptiles are what kind of initially

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.650
got my heart with animals
at a very young age.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.320
And I just love working
with them, and I love them.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.180
- Well, when I saw the cover of your book,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.440
that cover is so eye-catching.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.150
It is so bold.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.930
I mean, it is a bam, it is sexy.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:56.973
It's like, rrrl.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.620
- Oh, thank you. (laughing)

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.557
You're making me blush over here.

00:01:01.557 --> 00:01:04.430
- Will you please tell us about the book.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.330
- It's me in my early adulthood life,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.960
I guess, where I am a wildlife enthusiast,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.000
and I am so keen to just get out

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.690
into the wild and into
the natural habitats

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.150
where my favorite species are
native to, around the world.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.930
And that's places like
Australia and Africa

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.730
and Southeast Asia, Central America.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.010
And basically it's an evolution

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.970
of that kind of work and travel

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.170
into becoming a
veterinarian and being a vet

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:26.670
and getting to do real veterinary work

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:27.790
and real conservation work

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.390
and work with amazing
conservationists and vets

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.570
and wildlife rescues around the world

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.950
and getting to share my
story and the crazy travels.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.240
And there's happy moments
and exciting moments

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:38.920
and scary moments and sad moments

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.710
and funny moments and
everything in between.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:44.018
- When you were shooting
the cover of your book,

00:01:44.018 --> 00:01:46.980
did you bring in inspiration pictures?

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.350
Was there a nice Calvin Klein shot?

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.503
Like, you are giving it in this picture.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:57.520
It is like "World Wild Vet"
is like, it's so striking.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.200
- Yeah, no, I love that picture.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.630
And yeah, I just, I went
online and I Googled

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.000
just men holding giant
lizards on their shoulders.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.875
And I found so many great pictures.

00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:07.139
No, no, I'm kidding.

00:02:07.139 --> 00:02:09.050
It just, it just happened.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:10.360
And the photographer's awesome.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.910
I loved working with him.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.500
And we had a lot of great
shots from that day.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.530
And that lizard is,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.810
that was a very sweet
Asian water monitor lizard,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.260
and she was just cooperative.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.460
And I just, I don't know.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.900
I thought it'd be a fun idea, honestly.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.490
And I just, we were just
kind of playing with it,

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:26.678
and it turned out we got some good shots.

00:02:26.678 --> 00:02:30.220
- My daughter, Olive, her
nickname is ACE Ventura,

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.020
because she has that gift.
- Oh my gosh.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.960
- She has the gift with animals.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.000
And she's obsessed with them,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.350
and she wants to be a veterinarian.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.280
Do you have any advice for her?

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.630
- I think my biggest advice
would be just as much for you,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.311
and that's just to cultivate that.

00:02:44.311 --> 00:02:46.500
Get her books and shows and help her learn

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.620
and take her to the zoo,
good zoos and good trips,

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.910
and things like that, to get her exposed

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.849
and just really feed
that interest of hers.

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:55.930
- Dr. Evan Antin, we
have a question for you.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.160
Would it be okay?

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:02.160
Quianna, you have question for
the wonderful Dr. Evan Antin.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.543
- Yes. Hi there.

00:03:04.543 --> 00:03:06.310
- Hi, how you doing, Quianna?

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.690
- I'm good. This is Cupcake here.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.060
And as you can see, she's super tiny,

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.380
only about five pounds.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.100
And every time she goes for her checkup,

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:16.350
she's that same weight.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.460
But she eats so much. She's
always hungry. Why is that?

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.230
- That's a common thing in dogs.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.560
And Henry, to be honest, is the same way.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.020
I've got a little dog too.
- I love him.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.550
- And this guy will eat
until I stop feeding him.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:30.760
So I have to be really careful with that.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.520
And it really, our
metabolism works very similar

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.033
to that of dogs, in that
it all comes down to

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:38.570
how many calories are coming
in and how many calories

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.200
are being burned at the end of the day.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.890
And for dogs, if they're not as active

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:43.030
and they're kind of couch potatoes,

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.560
like Henry is very guilty of,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.143
and I'm guilty of basically
facilitating that,

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.640
it's easy for them to gain weight.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.210
And many dogs have high appetites.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.580
And then neutering and spaying dogs,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.350
which I highly encourage for most dogs,

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.720
is something that can actually
slow down the metabolism

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.380
and it can increase the appetite.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.190
Both things are kind of a
recipe for gaining weight.

00:04:04.190 --> 00:04:06.810
And so when you know all those things,

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.900
the key to it is just
make sure you're feeding

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.910
the appropriate amount for your pet,

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.660
and try to get them activity and exercise

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:15.400
if they're healthy enough to do so.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.830
- Well, I love what you do.

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.140
I've followed you for a long time.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.918
And I think whether it's you
on the cover of your book,

00:04:23.918 --> 00:04:27.090
the person that you are
that shines through,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.230
you help keep humans interested

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:33.257
in what's the responsible thing to do

00:04:33.257 --> 00:04:35.300
with animals and our planets.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.990
And I really thank you for
everything that you do.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:39.250
- Oh, that means so much.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.070
If that's your takeaway from
this book, that's everything.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:43.490
That makes me so happy to hear.

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:44.760
And that's exactly what I would love

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:45.850
for readers to get from it.

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:47.470
So those words mean a lot, Drew.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:48.798
Thank you very much.

00:04:48.798 --> 00:04:51.381
(upbeat music)

